Here's a simple JavaScript function that switches the order of adjectives in a sentence:
```javascript
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  const words = sentence.split(" ");
  const adjectives = words.filter(word => word.match(/^[a-z]+$/)); // Filter out non-adjective words
  const reversedAdjectives = adjectives.reverse();

  return reversedAdjectives.join(" ") + " " + words.filter(word => !word.match(/^[a-z]+$/)).join(" ");
}
// Example usage:
const sentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const result = switchAdjectives(sentence);
console.log(result); // Output: Green beautiful trees are in the park
```
This function first splits the input sentence into an array of words using the `split()` method. It then filters out the non-adjective words by checking if they match a regular expression pattern (`^[a-z]+$/`) which matches one or more lowercase letters. The filtered adjectives are then reversed and joined back into a string using the `reverse()`, `join()` methods respectively. Finally, the original sentence is split again into two arrays: one containing the adjectives and another containing the rest of the words. These arrays are then joined together to form the output sentence with the swapped adjectives.

```javascript
function swapAdjectives(sentence) {
  const words = sentence.split(' ');
  const adjectives = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
    if (words[i] === 'beautiful' || words[i] === 'green') {
      adjectives.push(words[i]);
    }
  }

  if (adjectives.length > 1) {
    const firstAdjective = adjectives.shift();
    adjectives.push(firstAdjective);
    words.splice(words.indexOf(firstAdjective), 1, ...adjectives);
  }

  return words.join(' ');
}

const inputSentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const outputSentence = swapAdjectives(inputSentence);
console.log(outputSentence); // Output: Green beautiful trees are in the park
```

