To make your circle element look more 3D, you can add some perspective and lighting effects using CSS. Here's an example:

```css
.circle {
  fill: radial-gradient(white, lightblue);
  filter: drop-shadow(15px 15px 15px gray);
  transform: translateZ(50px); /* Add depth */
  perspective: 1000px; /* Add perspective */
  backface-visibility: hidden; /* Hide back face */
  lighting: local; /* Enable local lighting */
  light-source: top; /* Place light source at top */
}
```

This will give your circle element a 3D effect by adding depth, perspective, and lighting. You can adjust the values of `translateZ`, `perspective`, `backface-visibility`, `lighting`, and `light-source` to achieve the desired effect.

Additionally, you can use CSS animations to create a spinning effect for your circle element. Here's an example:

```css
@keyframes spin {
  0% { transform: rotate(0deg); }
  100% { transform: rotate(360deg); }
}

.circle {
  animation: spin 2s infinite alternate; /* Spin every 2 seconds */
}
```

This will make your circle element spin continuously. You can adjust the duration and direction of the animation to achieve the desired effect.

