        }
    }
}
```

In this modified code, we use a try-catch block to handle the InputMismatchException. If an exception is thrown, we print an error message and consume the invalid input using `scanner.nextLine()`. Then, we recursively call the `main` method to prompt the user again. This will continue until the user enters a valid integer.

The error you're encountering is due to an input mismatch exception being thrown when trying to read an integer from the user using a Scanner object. This can happen if the user enters a non-numeric value or if there's an issue with the input stream.

To resolve this issue, you can add a try-catch block around the code that reads the integer input and handle the InputMismatchException appropriately. Here's an example of how you can modify your code:

```java
