Yes, you can tell if a message has been read in Slack API endpoints when retrieving a message by its ID. Slack provides a `read` field in the message object that indicates whether the message has been read or not. The value of this field is `true` if the message has been read and `false` if it hasn't.

Here's an example of how to retrieve a message and check if it has been read using the Slack API:
```javascript
const axios = require('axios');

async function getMessage(channelId, messageId) {
  const response = await axios.get(`https://slack.com/api/channels.history`, {
    params: {
      channel: channelId,
      count: 1,
      message_ts: messageId,
    },
  });

  const message = response.data.messages[0];
  return message;
}

async function main() {
  const channelId = 'C1234567';
  const messageId = '1234567890';

  try {
    const message = await getMessage(channelId, messageId);
    console.log('Message:', message);
    console.log('Read:', message.read);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error:', error);
  }
}

main();
```
In this example, we use the `axios` library to make a GET request to the Slack API's `channels.history` endpoint with the `channel`, `count`, and `message_ts` parameters set to the appropriate values. We then parse the response and log the message object and its `read` field to the console.

