Note that this code only supports the most common tense forms in English. It may not work correctly for more complex or irregular sentences.

Here's a simple MATLAB code to identify the tense used in a given sentence:

```matlab
function tense = identify_tense(sentence)
    % Split the sentence into words
    words = strsplit(sentence, ' ');

    % Check for common tense markers
    if strcmpi(words{end}, 'would') || strcmpi(words{end}, 'could') || strcmpi(words{end}, 'should')
        tense = 'conditional';
    elseif strcmpi(words{end}, 'is') || strcmpi(words{end}, 'are') || strcmpi(words{end}, 'was') || strcmpi(words{end}, 'were')
        tense = 'present';
    elseif strcmpi(words{end}, 'been')
        tense = 'past perfect';
    elseif strcmpi(words{end}, 'going')
        tense = 'future';
    else
        tense = 'unknown';
    end
end
```

To use this function, simply call it with your input sentence as an argument:

```matlab
input_sentence = 'I would like to have a meeting today';
output_tense = identify_tense(input_sentence);
disp(['The tense of the sentence is: ', output_tense]);
```

This code will output:

```
The tense of the sentence is: conditional
```

Keep in mind that this is a simplified approach and may not work perfectly for all sentences. For more accurate results, you might want to consider using a natural language processing library such as NLTK or SpaCy.

Here's an example of a generic REST service wrapper class in C# with authentication token handling:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Newtonsoft.Json.Linq;

public class RestServiceWrapper<T>
{
    private readonly string _baseUrl;
    private readonly string _authToken;

    public RestServiceWrapper(string baseUrl, string authToken)
    {
        _baseUrl = baseUrl;
        _authToken = authToken;
    }

    public async Task<T> GetAsync()
    {
        using (var client = new HttpClient())
        {
            client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Authorization = new System.Net.Http.Headers.AuthenticationHeaderValue("Bearer", _authToken);

            var response = await client.GetAsync(_baseUrl);
            response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();

            return await response.Content.ReadAsAsync<T>();
        }
    }
}

public class MyRestService : RestServiceWrapper<JObject>
{
    public MyRestService(string baseUrl, string authToken) : base(baseUrl, authToken) {}

    public async Task<JObject> GetDataAsync()
    {
        return await base.GetAsync();
    }
}
```

In this example, the `RestServiceWrapper` class takes a base URL and an authentication token as constructors parameters. The `GetAsync` method makes a GET request to the specified URL with the provided authentication token. The response is then deserialized into the expected type (in this case, JObject).

The `MyRestService` class inherits from `RestServiceWrapper` and overrides the `GetDataAsync` method to return a specific type of data (in this case, JObject). You can create other classes like `MyRestService` for different types of responses.

Here's an example of a generic REST service wrapper class in C# that includes auth token handling:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Newtonsoft.Json;
using System.Text;

public class RestServiceWrapper<T>
{
    private readonly string _baseUrl;
    private readonly string _authToken;

    public RestServiceWrapper(string baseUrl, string authToken)
    {
        _baseUrl = baseUrl;
        _authToken = authToken;
    }

    public async Task<T> GetAsync(string path)
    {
        using (var client = new HttpClient())
        {
            client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Authorization = new System.Net.Http.Headers.AuthenticationHeaderValue("Bearer", _authToken);
            var response = await client.GetAsync($"{_baseUrl}/{path}");
            response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
            return await response.Content.ReadAsAsync<T>();
        }
    }

    public async Task<T> PostAsync(string path, T data)
    {
        using (var client = new HttpClient())
        {
            client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Authorization = new System.Net.Http.Headers.AuthenticationHeaderValue("Bearer", _authToken);
            var content = new StringContent(JsonConvert.SerializeObject(data), Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");
            var response = await client.PostAsync($"{_baseUrl}/{path}", content);
            response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
            return await response.Content.ReadAsAsync<T>();
        }
    }
}
```

To use this class, create an instance of it with the base URL and auth token, and then call the `GetAsync` or `PostAsync` methods to make requests to the REST service.

