To retrieve the username from the JWT token in ASP.NET C# WebAPI, you can use the `JwtSecurityTokenHandler` class to parse the token and extract the claim containing the username. Here's an example:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt;
using System.Security.Claims;

public static string GetUsernameFromJwtToken(string jwtToken)
{
    var tokenHandler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
    var key = Encoding.ASCII.Parse("your_secret_key");
    var token = tokenHandler.ReadJwtToken(jwtToken, new TokenValidationParameters
    {
        IssuerSigningKey = key,
        ValidateIssuer = false,
        ValidateAudience = false,
        ValidateLifetime = true,
        ClockSkew = TimeSpan.Zero
    });

    var usernameClaim = token.Claims.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Type == "username");
    if (usernameClaim != null)
    {
        return usernameClaim.Value;
    }
    else
    {
        return null;
    }
}
```

Replace `"your_secret_key"` with your secret key used to sign the JWT token. This method will return the username claim value if it exists in the token, otherwise it will return `null`.

To retrieve the username from the JWT token in ASP.NET C# WebApi, you can use the `ClaimsPrincipal` class and the `User` property. Here's an example:

```csharp
using Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens;
using System;
using System.Security.Claims;

public class JwtTokenService
{
    public string GetUsernameFromJwtToken(string token)
    {
        var tokenHandler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
        var key = Encoding.ASCII.Parse("your_secret_key");
        var tokenValidationParameters = new TokenValidationParameters
        {
            IssuerSigningKey = key,
            ValidateIssuer = false,
            ValidateAudience = false,
            ValidateLifetime = true,
            RequireExpirationTime = true
        };

        var securityToken = tokenHandler.ReadJwtToken(token, tokenValidationParameters);
        var claims = securityToken.Claims;

        foreach (var claim in claims)
        {
            if (claim.Type == "http://schemas.microsoft.com/identity/claims/username")
            {
                return claim.Value;
            }
        }

        return null;
    }
}
```

Replace `"your_secret_key"` with your secret key used to sign the JWT token. This method takes a JWT token as input and returns the username claimed in the token. If no username is found, it returns null.

To retrieve the username from the JWT token in ASP.NET C# WebAPI, you can follow these steps:

1. First, make sure you have installed the `Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens` package using NuGet.
2. Next, create a custom middleware class that will extract the username from the JWT token. Here's an example:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Security.Claims;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens;

public class UsernameMiddleware
{
    private readonly RequestDelegate _next;
    private readonly TokenValidationParameters _tokenValidationParameters;

    public UsernameMiddleware(RequestDelegate next, TokenValidationParameters tokenValidationParameters)
    {
        _next = next;
        _tokenValidationParameters = tokenValidationParameters;
    }

    public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext context)
    {
        var token = context.Request.Headers["Authorization"].FirstOrDefault();
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(token))
        {
            await _next(context);
            return;
        }

        try
        {
            var tokenHandler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
            var securityToken = tokenHandler.ReadJwtToken(token, _tokenValidationParameters);
            var username = securityToken.Claims.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Type == ClaimTypes.Name).Value;

            context.Items["Username"] = username;
            await _next(context);
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            context.Response.StatusCode = StatusCodes.Status401Unauthorized;
            await context.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(new { error = "Invalid token" });
        }
    }
}
```
3. Next, add the middleware to the `Configure` method in your Startup.cs file:
```csharp
public void Configure(IApplicationBuilder app, IWebHostEnvironment env)
{
    app.UseMiddleware<UsernameMiddleware>();

    // ...
}
```
