Sure! Here's a simple MATLAB code that simulates natural selection:

```matlab
% Define the population size, number of generations, and initial population
population_size = 100;
num_generations = 100;
initial_population = randi([0 1], population_size, 1); % Randomly generate initial population

% Define the fitness function
fitness_function = @(x) sum(x); % Fitness is proportional to the sum of the values in the vector

% Simulate natural selection
for i = 1:num_generations
    % Evaluate fitness of each individual
    fitness_values = fitness_function(initial_population);

    % Select parents based on fitness
    selected_parents = initial_population(sort(fitness_values, 'descend'), :);

    % Create offspring by combining genes of parents
    offspring = selected_parents * 0.5 + (1 - selected_parents) * 0.5; % Assuming a 50% crossover rate

    % Mutate offspring with a small probability
    mutation_probability = 0.01;
    mutated_offspring = offspring .* (1 - mutation_probability) + mutation_probability * randn(size(offspring));

    % Update the population for the next generation
    initial_population = mutated_offspring;
end

% Plot the evolution of the population over time
figure;
plot(1:num_generations, mean(initial_population, 2));
xlabel('Generation');
ylabel('Average Fitness');
title('Natural Selection Simulation');
```

This code simulates a simple population of individuals with a fitness function that measures the sum of their values. The population evolves over time through selection, crossover, and mutation, with the fittest individuals having a higher chance of passing on their genes to the next generation. The plot shows the evolution of the average fitness of the population over time.

