It seems like the issue might be related to the 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header being set to '\*', which allows requests from any origin. When you try to include an authorization header, it might be getting overridden by the wildcard value.

To resolve this issue, you can try setting the 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header to a specific domain or a list of domains that you trust. This will allow requests only from those origins and prevent unauthorized access.

Alternatively, you can also use a more fine-grained approach by implementing CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) with pre-flight requests. This will allow you to specify which headers are allowed to be included in the request, including the authorization header.

Here's an example of how you can implement CORS with pre-flight requests using Node.js and Express:
```javascript
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

app.use(express.json());

app.options('/api/resource', (req, res) => {
  res.status(200).header({
    'Access-Control-Allow-Methods': 'GET, POST, PUT, DELETE',
    'Access-Control-Allow-Headers': 'Authorization, Content-Type',
    'Access-Control-Allow-Credentials': true
  }).send();
});

app.get('/api/resource', (req, res) => {
  res.status(200).send('Hello, world!');
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server started on port 3000');
});
```
In this example, we've created an options route for the '/api/resource' endpoint that handles pre-flight requests. We're allowing GET, POST, PUT, DELETE methods, and setting the 'Access-Control-Allow-Headers' to 'Authorization, Content-Type'. We're also allowing credentials (if needed).

When a client makes a request to this endpoint, it will first send a pre-flight request to check if the request is allowed. If the request is allowed, the client will send the actual request with the authorization header included.

