To prevent the planets from being too close together, you can implement a simple distance check between each planet before adding it to the array. If the distance between two planets is less than a certain threshold, you can generate a new random position for one of the planets until they are far enough apart. Here's an updated version of your `generateRandomPlanets` function:
```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        return Math.abs(coord - planet.position[index]) < minDistance;
      })
    );

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    } else {
      attempts--;
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```
In this updated version, we added a `minDistance` parameter to the function, which specifies the minimum distance between two planets. We then check if the distance between the new planet and any existing planet is less than this threshold. If it is, we decrease the `attempts` counter and continue generating new planets until we find one that is far enough away from the others.

You can adjust the value of `minDistance` to control the spacing between the planets. A smaller value will result in more closely spaced planets, while a larger value will create more space between them.

