To create a React utility to remove widows, you can use the `useEffect` hook to listen for changes in the component's width and adjust the text accordingly. Here's a simple example:

```jsx
import React, { useEffect } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ children }) => {
  useEffect(() => {
    const adjustText = () => {
      const textNodes = document.querySelectorAll('p');
      textNodes.forEach(node => {
        node.style.widows = '0';
      });
    };

    window.addEventListener('resize', adjustText);
    return () => {
      window.removeEventListener('resize', adjustText);
    };
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>{children}</div>
  );
};

export default RemoveWidows;
```

This component will listen for window resize events and adjust the `widows` property of all `p` elements within the component. You can wrap your content with this component to remove widows automatically.

Here's a simple React utility that removes widows (lines that are too long and break the layout) from a given text. This utility uses the `wordWrap` CSS property to wrap words within a specific width, and then adjusts the line height to ensure that all lines have the same height.

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const RemoveWidows = ({ text, maxWidth }) => {
  const [wrappedText, setWrappedText] = useState('');

  const handleChange = (event) => {
    const newText = event.target.value;
    setWrappedText(newText);
  };

  const wrapText = () => {
    const lines = wrappedText.split('\n');
    const lineHeights = [];

    lines.forEach((line, index) => {
      const lineWidth = line.length * 10; // Assuming each character is 10 pixels wide
      const lineHeight = 16; // Assuming a default line height of 16 pixels

      if (lineWidth > maxWidth) {
        const words = line.split(' ');
        const lastWordIndex = words.length - 1;

        for (let i = lastWordIndex; i >= 0; i--) {
          const word = words[i];
          const newLine = `${words.slice(0, i).join(' ')}\n${word}`;

          if (newLine.length <= maxWidth) {
            lines[index] = newLine;
            break;
          }
        }
      }

      lineHeights.push(lineHeight);
    });

    const adjustedLineHeight = Math.max(...lineHeights) + 4; // Adding a bit of extra space between lines
    const wrappedTextWithLineHeights = lines.join(`\n<span style="line-height: ${adjustedLineHeight}px;">`);

    return wrappedTextWithLineHeights;
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <textarea value={wrappedText} onChange={handleChange} />
      <div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: wrapText() }} />
    </div>

Here's a simple React utility to remove widows in your text. It will split the text into sentences and check if any sentence is wider than the container width. If it is, it will wrap the sentence in a `div` element with a class name of `widow`. You can then style the `widow` class to adjust the layout as needed.

```jsx
import React from 'react';

const WidowRemover = ({ text, containerWidth }) => {
  const sentences = text.split('.');
  const widowedSentences = [];

  sentences.forEach((sentence) => {
    const words = sentence.split(' ');
    const sentenceWidth = words.reduce((acc, word) => acc + word.length, 0);

    if (sentenceWidth > containerWidth) {
      widowedSentences.push(sentence);
    }
  });

  return (
    <div>
      {sentences.map((sentence, index) => (
        <React.Fragment key={index}>
          {widowedSentences.includes(sentence) ? (
            <div className="widow">{sentence}</div>
          ) : (
            sentence
          )}
          <br />
        </React.Fragment>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
};

export default WidowRemover;
```

